<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="phosphate acquisition" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6348" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Plant growth and development requires macro and micronutrients, some of which are available only from external sources like the soil in which they grow. Phosphorous is one such essential macronutrient, it is usually absorbed and utilized by the plants in the form of |FRAME: Pi| (also known as inorganic phosphate, or Pi) |CITS: [18713395]|. Many soils are deficient in |FRAME: Pi|, leading to |FRAME: Pi| starvation for plants growing in them. Plants develop several strategies to overcome |FRAME: Pi| limitation, including morphological, biochemical and physiological responses to enhance the acquisition of this vital macronutrient. These combined responses enhance the chances of plants to survive the prospects of |FRAME: Pi| starvation. Due to continous leaching, ancient soils are known to become limited in the availability of |FRAME: Pi|. Once absorbed, the |FRAME: Pi| is utilized inside the cells at optimum cellular concentration for the various developmental and biochemical processes. Phosphate acquisition is a complicated process and has various modified routes. The |FRAME: Pi| homeostasis is maintained by acquisition, limited consumption and effective recycling, when the supply and demand is not equitable. The molecular basis for these strategies is being studied in a number of species |CITS: [19168668]|. The Gulf Ryegrass is an ideal model plant as it hyperaccumulates |FRAME: Pi| utilizing multiple acquisition and mobilization systems under varying |FRAME: Pi| regimes |CITS: [1882180]|. In some plants the chief enzymes that partcipate in the transport and recycling of |FRAME: Pi| are developmentally regulated |CITS: [12021284]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The Purple Acid Phosphatases (PAP&apos;s) are said to play a role in |FRAME: Pi| salvaging when cells are depleted or starved of |FRAME: Pi| and under oxidative stress. Thus PAP&apos;s are crucial for the cellular metabolism and energy transduction processes. PAP&apos;s have also been isolated from mammalian and fungal sources, although the physiological function of these enzymes has yet to be established |CITS: [10510276]|. Genes encoding such enzymes are also present in some bacteria, including cyanobacteria, but their role is not clear. It has been suggested that they prevent damage caused by generated by invaded host cells (mycobacteria) or by the light-harvesting complex (cyanobacteria) |CITS: [11024303]|. However, the enzyme of |FRAME: TAX-216591| is induced by lack of phosphate and excreted from the cell, suggesting it may be involved in phosphate acquisition |CITS: [19376213]|. The PAP enzymes may exist in two forms: the inactive purple form, where the redox-active iron is in the ferric state, and the active pink form, where the iron is reduced to the ferrous state.The PAP&apos;s are expressed in a tissue specific manner and often exist as isozymes |CITS: [16244908][15375722 ]|. The isozymes exhibit variation in molecular structure, substrate specficity and localization. Some PAP&apos;s, such as |FRAME: MONOMER-15162| from |FRAME: TAX-4081|, are multifunctional enzymes that have both a purple acid phosphatase and an alkaline peroxidase function. These enzymes are hypothesized to function in the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) |CITS: [12473124]|. The phosphatase and oxygen radical-generating activities of PAP&apos;s are functionally independent, similar to those of mammalian PAP |CITS: [11890682]|.</Description>
